# Manuscript PDF page 166

                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “Doña Rosa. Forty-one dollars. Señor Pagán. Eighty. The Quiñones
family, three-sixty—”
   He tore a page out of the ledger. It came away with a long, dry rip.
   “Doña Rosa.” He held it out. “Estás saldo.”
   You’re settled.
   A stamped word across the page, in red. PAGADO. Paid.
   She took it, a tiny woman in a church hat, and put her hand over her
mouth.
   He did it for every name. Page after page. The wreaths shook. A kid
cried. A man in a work shirt shook Gus’s hand and wouldn’t let go. In
the corner booth, almost invisible, sat a man in a short-sleeved shirt
and a tie with a notebook open on the table. He was counting, I could
see his pencil move, ticking off every torn page like a man watching
money leave the building. He looked up once, puzzled, at Gus, at the
whole room, at something he didn’t understand and couldn’t put in a
column.
   Then the Olympia came back, and a plate of eggs, and a boy of
twenty-five across from me with tears standing in his eyes.
   “What?” said Mikey.
   “Nothing.” I wiped my face with my sleeve. “Your tab’s settled.”
   “What?”
   “Gus. Every Christmas. He tears out the pages.” I laughed, a wet, ri‐
diculous sound. “That’s the other ledger.”



Then I told him.
   I said it badly, in pieces, over a plate of cold home fries. I said I’d
been awake that night. I said I’d seen the words arrive and put the
phone down and looked at the ceiling, and that I had a whole explana‐
tion ready. I’d been tired. I’d been ashamed. Every conversation with
him had started to feel like an invoice. I’d learned some words lately
that could dress the excuse up as a diagnosis.
   I didn’t say any of it.
   I said, “I was awake, and I didn’t answer, and I’m sorry.”




